    <body>
      <sec id="article-17551.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Pediatric cerebral aneurysms are a rare but clinically significant cause of subarachnoid hemorrhage in children. This course outlines this condition, which, although accounting for only 0.5% to 5% of all aneurysms, differs markedly from adult aneurysms in etiology, often involving congenital vessel wall defects, connective tissue disorders, trauma, or infection rather than acquired vascular risk factors. Participants will review the evolving understanding of aneurysm pathophysiology, including structural abnormalities of the arterial wall and the interplay between intrinsic defects and external insults.</p>
        <p>The activity also&#x000a0;reviews key differences in the clinical presentation of pediatric cerebral aneurysms, such as higher rates of rupture at diagnosis and unique manifestations including mass effect and hydrocephalus. Diagnostic strategies, including advanced imaging and angiography, as well as medical, surgical, and endovascular management approaches, are also discussed.&#x000a0;This activity for healthcare professionals is designed to enhance the learner's competence in identifying pediatric cerebral aneurysms, performing the recommended evaluation, applying risk stratification, and implementing an appropriate interprofessional approach when managing this condition,&#x000a0;to optimize outcomes in this vulnerable population.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify clinical features associated with pediatric&#x000a0;cerebral aneurysms to support timely diagnosis.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Differentiate pediatric from adult cerebral aneurysms based on&#x000a0;diagnostic features to guide appropriate management decisions.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Apply evidence-based management strategies to optimize outcomes in patients with&#x000a0;pediatric&#x000a0;cerebral&#x000a0;aneurysms.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Coordinate interprofessional care among team members to improve collaboration&#x000a0;and continuity of care in patients with&#x000a0;pediatric&#x000a0;cerebral&#x000a0;aneurysms.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=17551&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=17551">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-17551.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>One of the earliest recorded cases of a childhood aneurysm was a 15-year-old boy reported by the German pathologist Eppinger in 1871. Postmortem examination revealed stenosis of the aortic isthmus along with the unusual finding of free blood at the base of the brain arising from a ruptured saccular aneurysm of the right anterior cerebral artery.&#x000a0;Edvard Bull subsequently described the first antemortem case of a 17-year-old girl who presented with a severe headache and third nerve palsy. The clinical diagnosis of a ruptured posterior communicating artery aneurysm was subsequently confirmed by autopsy. The rarity of pediatric aneurysms was further described in the early to mid-20th century, when several large series in the United States and Europe reported that between 0.5% and 5% of all aneurysms occurred in children.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Whereas &#x0201c;acquired&#x0201d; factors, eg, diabetes, alcohol, hypertension, high-fat and cholesterol diets, obesity, cigarette consumption, and oral contraceptive use have correlations with subarachnoid hemorrhage (SAH) in adults, such influences are minimal or altogether absent in children. Several authors have postulated the greater role of congenital factors in the development of childhood aneurysms. As microsurgical techniques developed in the latter half of the 20th century, mortality from pediatric aneurysm rupture declined, and despite the continued rarity of pediatric aneurysm rupture, high-volume centers noted that the overall course in young patients was generally less morbid, with fewer occurrences of vasospasm and delayed ischemia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;The differences in etiology, presentation, and clinical course between adult and pediatric aneurysms necessitate a thorough understanding of pediatric cerebral aneurysms&#x000a0;as an entity in their own right to more safely inform management decisions regarding screening, surgery, and critical care after rupture.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-17551.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>
<bold>Idiopathic Aneurysms</bold>
</p>
        <p>Significant controversy exists as to the exact nature of idiopathic aneurysm formation. Whether aneurysm development is a question of nature versus nurture remains unanswered. Postmortem studies of the cerebral vasculature of aneurysm patients have revealed multiple defects in the media at multiple arterial bifurcations. Histological exam routinely demonstrates a fragmentation of both the internal elastic membrane and the muscularis layer of the media. Within the aneurysm pouch, the smooth muscle is generally&#x000a0;absent, but the internal elastic membrane may continue for a variable length into the neck of the lesion. The basement membrane underneath the endothelium is also frequently thickened and reticulated. What remains within the wall is typically an endothelial lining, fibrous tissue, adventitia, inflammatory infiltrates, and hemorrhage of varying ages. Calcifications are also commonly encountered. The site of rupture is usually at the apex of the lesion, with a small diverticulum often seen at the site of perforation. Most reported cases of pediatric aneurysm seem to indicate that the histology of childhood lesions resembles that of adult saccular aneurysms.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r8">[8]</xref></p>
        <p>Saccular aneurysms most often occur at the bifurcation of the internal carotid artery or one of its intermediate branches. Not only are these branching points physically more susceptible to injury as a result of high shear forces, but pathologic analysis has also demonstrated that the number of internal elastic membrane fenestrations is frequently also highest at these areas with an injury to the internal elastic lamina. Usually, the initial pathophysiological alteration leads to aneurysm formation.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>What is less clear is the exact mechanism leading to this fragmentation of the elastica. Whereas developmental defects of the muscularis layer of the media are a common finding in the cerebral arteries of normal patients, their frequency is still far greater than the incidence of aneurysm formation. Crompton, in a summary of 149 autopsies, noted that the size and the number of these medial gaps appear to increase with age.</p>
        <p>Aneurysms in childhood have been associated with various collagen vascular diseases and connective tissue disorders. The cerebral vasculature is distinguished from systemic vessels by the thinness of its walls and lack of perivascular support. Type I and type III collagen predominate in the intracranial circulation and provide the major load-bearing capabilities of the blood vessels. Approximately half of adult aneurysm patients lack or have deficient levels of type III collagen. Similar findings have also been observed in the aneurysm walls of young children. &#x000d8;stergaard has postulated that because type III collagen deficiency produces an increased distensibility of the vessel wall, the larger size of childhood aneurysms could be explained by the occurrence of a more compliant aneurysm wall lacking in such fibers.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r10">[10]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r11">[11]</xref></p>
        <p>Another early theory proposed that childhood aneurysms arose from remnants of small vascular trunks stemming from arterial bifurcations. According to this theory, saccular aneurysms arose from the stumps of vessels that disappear when the fetal cerebrovascular network matures to form the adult system of major arterial trunks. Such vestigial or incompletely aborted nubbins could become progressively dilated to form aneurysms&#x000a0;over time. Isolated reports have described children with aneurysms in which only a medial defect with an intact&#x000a0;elastica&#x000a0;was present. Nonetheless, substantial corroborating evidence for this theory is lacking to date. Given the histologic and clinical features of childhood aneurysms, the hypothesis that these lesions are mainly congenital is attractive. On the contrary, Stehbens, in his careful analysis of the literature, reviewed the age incidence, associated conditions, prevalence of hypertension and atherosclerosis, histology, and electron microscopic structure of childhood cases and concluded that a purely degenerative pathogenetic model could explain aneurysm formation in these children. Early damage from hypertension, birth injury, trauma, and in utero insults may play a significant role in such cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r12">[12]</xref></p>
        <p>Atheromatous degeneration and hemodynamic factors are thought to further weaken the vessels in congenitally predisposed patients. However, in this author's experience and in the literature, most children with intracranial aneurysms have little or no atheromatous change near the aneurysm. The majority of children diagnosed with aneurysms are normotensive. However, childhood arterial hypertension does exist as a secondary phenomenon in children with aneurysms who also have an aortic coarctation or polycystic kidney disease. Though the coexistence of these processes with aneurysms may be fortuitous, the possibility that arterial hypertension contributes to aneurysm formation in these children cannot be excluded.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r13">[13]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Traumatic Aneurysms</bold>
</p>
        <p>In cases where a history of closed head injury is present, a traumatic injury to the vessel wall may lead to subsequent aneurysm formation. Such injury of the arterial wall may occur with increased frequency at certain locations within the cranium, thus leading to the clinical observation that traumatic childhood aneurysms often occur at stereotypical sites. These include the anterior cerebral artery distribution and its distal branches resulting from injury along the falx, the posterior cerebral artery near the free edge of the tentorium cerebelli, and the middle cerebral artery along its course on the sphenoid ridge.</p>
        <p>Some authors have postulated a traumatic origin for many lesions in unusual locations characteristic of childhood cases. For example, an increased prevalence of P2 segment aneurysms has frequently been reported in children. Meyer et al postulated that these lesions might develop during periods of increased intracranial pressure early in development (ie, during delivery) when herniation of the posterior cerebral artery over the tentorium could cause vessel injury.&#x000a0;True traumatic aneurysms have only an intact adventitial layer in their walls, with the remaining normal structures disrupted. False traumatic aneurysms result from a fibrous organization around a prior hematoma encasing the parent vessel. False lesions often develop after direct penetrating injury, as in the case of depressed skull fractures or of penetrating injuries, eg, gunshot and stab wounds. The histology of these lesions ultimately has little bearing on their clinical management.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r14">[14]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r15">[15]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r16">[16]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r17">[17]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Infectious Aneurysms</bold>
</p>
        <p>Originally termed &#x0201c;mycotic&#x0201d; aneurysms by Sir William Osler in 1885, the majority of infectious childhood aneurysms result from bacterial rather than fungal infection, the latter of which is quite&#x000a0;rare. Mielke, in his comprehensive review of the literature up to 1981, found only 13 confirmed cases of fungal intracranial aneurysms.&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Aspergillus, Candida</italic>, and <italic toggle="yes">Phycomycetes</italic> species were, in descending order, most commonly implicated. With few exceptions, the patients were immunocompromised with a near&#x000a0;100% mortality rate after diagnosis of their aneurysm.</p>
        <p>Karsner, in his analysis of infectious aneurysms, classified them into 3 primary forms. The first, being the most frequent, occurs in the setting of bacterial endocarditis with subsequent embolization; this is commonly described as the &#x0201c;intravascular&#x0201d; variety of an infectious aneurysm. The second type involves a direct extension from an adjacent nidus of infection. This extravascular source may be secondary to meningitis, cavernous sinus thrombophlebitis, adjacent osteomyelitis, sinus or postoperative infection. For such lesions, the anatomical location of the proximal carotid and other large proximal vessels at the base of the brain makes them particularly vulnerable to injury. The third group requires a diagnosis of an infectious aneurysm with stereotypical radiologic and histologic findings, in the absence of any other obvious inflammatory lesion elsewhere.</p>
        <p>Alpha-hemolytic<italic toggle="yes">&#x000a0;</italic>streptococci,<italic toggle="yes"> Staphylococcus, Pseudomonas,&#x000a0;</italic>and<italic toggle="yes">&#x000a0;Haemophilus&#x000a0;</italic>species are most frequently encountered in children with bacterial intracranial aneurysms. The interval from the embolic event to the time of aneurysm formation can be surprisingly&#x000a0;short, with the entire course of embolism, local arteritis, aneurysm formation, and hemorrhage usually occurring within 2 days in Molinari&#x02019;s experimental animal models. Necrosis and destruction proceeded from the adventitial surface to the media, with the internal elastic membrane and intima being involved last.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r19">[19]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r20">[20]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-17551.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>Pediatric autopsy series reveals no incidental cases of cerebral aneurysms in 3000 pediatric autopsies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r21">[21]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r22">[22]</xref>&#x000a0;Literature review of clinical series supports this rarity. Pasqualin et al reported 38 cases of&#x000a0;SAH in pediatric patients, which represented only 2.6% of their total number of ruptured aneurysms during the same time period at their institution.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;A combined British cohort in the mid-20th century reported only 58 pediatric cases of 3000 (1.9%).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;A study from Finland around the same time reported 35 pediatric cases of 1175 (2.9%).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r23">[23]</xref>&#x000a0;Whether this rarity is due to a lower likelihood of rupture and, consequently, underdiagnosis in pediatric patients remains unknown.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Demographics and Location</bold>
</p>
        <p>Childhood aneurysms most often become symptomatic from birth to age 6 and then from 8 years to adolescence. A peak in the first&#x000a0;6 months characterizes the early childhood group, with the majority of cases occurring within the first&#x000a0;2 years of life. Early childhood cases have also been associated with a higher incidence of lesions localized along the middle cerebral artery and its distal branches, as well as involving the vertebrobasilar system.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-17551.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>Similar to their adult counterparts, aneurysms of childhood have associations and links with a variety of systemic and intracranial disorders. Whereas some may represent purely coincidental occurrences, other conditions, eg, aortic coarctation, are generally acknowledged as bearing an increased risk for a cerebral aneurysm. The most common of such conditions include coarctation of the aorta, polycystic renal disease, fibromuscular dysplasia, tuberous sclerosis, Ehlers-Danlos syndrome, Marfan syndrome, and familial syndromes.&#x000a0;Aside from the more common arteriopathies already mentioned, both syphilis and pseudoxanthoma elasticum have also been involved in cases of childhood SAH.</p>
        <p>Azarelli reported the case of a child with a history of prior curative irradiation for intracranial germinoma who subsequently developed multiple aneurysms and expired from a ruptured fusiform lesion of the ICA. Moyamoya disease also has correlations with an increased risk of multiple childhood aneurysms.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r24">[24]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r25">[25]</xref>&#x000a0;As&#x000a0;prior irradiation has been implicated in the development of Moyamoya syndrome, one could postulate that Azarelli&#x02019;s case might have fit into this category. Other vascular structural anomalies include abnormal or persistent fetal patterns or vestigial remnants that frequently present in children with aneurysms.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r26">[26]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r27">[27]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r28">[28]</xref></p>
        <p>Intracranial aneurysms also correlate with various hematologic diseases in children. Several cases of infants and young children with maternally acquired human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) have developed aneurysms of the proximal circulation. Whether these lesions represent direct injury to the vessel wall by the retrovirus itself or are associated with infectious conditions remains unclear. An unusually high incidence of multiple aneurysms in HIV cases has been reported. Furthermore, aneurysms associated with noninfectious hematologic disorders, eg, thalassemia minor, sickle cell disease, and&#x000a0;glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency, have been observed.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Isolated case reports of cerebral aneurysms in children with concurrent cerebral tumors are also present in the literature. From&#x000a0;a current series, Zee reported a fascinating case of a young boy with a temporal MCA aneurysm encased in an anaplastic sarcoma of the temporal lobe. Along with several other authors, we have also observed a child whose&#x000a0;aneurysm&#x000a0;was diagnosed upon evaluation for hypopituitarism secondary to a pituitary adenoma. Reports also exist of patients with cardiac myxoma and multiple intracranial aneurysms.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Traumatic Aneurysms</bold>
</p>
        <p>Traumatic aneurysms typically develop from a direct penetrating injury or in association with a closed head injury. Classically, the location of these lesions in children is related to the direct path of the penetrating missile, to vascular suspensory regions of the brain, or to areas of vascular contiguity with dural margins. Peripheral cortical vessels are often directly involved below the edges of a fracture of the cranium.</p>
        <p>Ventureya classified traumatic aneurysms into 4 major groups. The first group includes lesions of the internal carotid artery, further subdivided into cavernous, supraclinoid, and petrous types, which are the most commonly encountered; supraclinoid aneurysms remain rare.&#x000a0;The second group consists of lesions within the anterior cerebral artery circulation, typically located distal to the anterior communicating artery. The third group encompasses aneurysms in the middle cerebral artery distribution, usually located near or distal to the artery's bifurcation. The fourth group includes aneurysms located near the vertebrobasilar confluence. Multiple traumatic aneurysms occur with the lowest frequency.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r29">[29]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r30">[30]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r15">[15]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Giant Aneurysms</bold>
</p>
        <p>Saccular aneurysms exceeding 25 mm in maximal diameter have traditionally been classified as &#x0201c;giant.&#x0201d; Whether these lesions arise through dynamic evolution from smaller aneurysms with intermittent wall breakdown remains uncertain. Across aneurysmal series spanning all age groups, giant lesions account for approximately 2% to 5% of cases. In children, giant aneurysms occur much more frequently. Reported proportions of large or giant lesions include 45% in the series by Amacher and Drake, 54% by Meyer and Sundt, 20% by Gerosa et al, and 28.5% by Humphreys. A review of 500 childhood cases identified 20% as giant aneurysms. Among children younger than 5 years, the proportion increases further, with 50% classified as large and 30% as giant. Anecdotal reports also describe massive aneurysms in very young infants. Although referral bias influences several series, published data suggest an increasing frequency of giant aneurysms with younger age.</p>
        <p>Adult giant intracranial aneurysms demonstrate a different distribution compared with smaller lesions. Approximately 40% occur in the carotid distribution, 25% in the anterior and middle cerebral artery territories, and 30% in the vertebrobasilar circulation, reflecting a 6-fold increase in posterior fossa involvement. Pediatric giant aneurysms share a predilection for the posterior circulation, with an even greater tendency. Hacker&#x02019;s review of 500 cases noted an approximately equal distribution between anterior and posterior systems. In our series of 52 children, 12 had giant aneurysms, including 6 located in the vertebrobasilar territory, and 8 patients were male. While 60% of adult giant aneurysms occur in women, 62% of pediatric giant aneurysms occur in males.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r31">[31]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r32">[32]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Coarctation of the Aorta</bold>
</p>
        <p>One of the earliest descriptions of a ruptured pediatric aneurysm involved a 15-year-old boy with a stenotic lesion of the aortic isthmus. As Matson highlighted, the association between aortic coarctation and ruptured cerebral aneurysms in children has been consistently documented throughout the past century. In 1927, postmortem examinations of 32 children with aortic coarctation identified cerebral hemorrhage as the cause of death in 37.5% of cases. The following year, Abbott reported that 7 of 20 children with cerebral hemorrhage and coarctation had confirmed ruptured cerebral aneurysms on autopsy.</p>
        <p>Estimating the true incidence of cerebral aneurysms in children with aortic coarctation proves challenging because most affected children remain asymptomatic until later in life. In a review of 200 patients with coarctation, Tyler and Clark reported only 5 patients diagnosed with cerebral aneurysms during life, with an average age of rupture at 25 years. Neurologic manifestations often provide the first clues to coarctation. LeBlanc noted that in young, hypertensive, noncyanosed patients, intermittent neurological symptoms or sudden cerebral insults may indicate underlying aortic coarctation. He further confirmed the association of coarctation with ruptured cerebral aneurysms and highlighted the predilection for multiple aneurysms in these patients. Clinicians should evaluate for a continuous murmur, arterial hypertension proximal to the stenosis, and diminished distal pulses with hypotension.</p>
        <p>Complications of coarctation extend beyond aneurysmal rupture. Elevated arterial pressure can result in intracerebral hemorrhage, emboli arising from associated aortic aneurysms or surgical sites may produce stroke, and infected emboli can lead to cerebral abscess. Surgical relief of aortic stenosis often reduces proximal blood pressure and may lower the risk of intracerebral hemorrhage and aneurysmal rupture. Some authors advocate for surgical repair of cerebral aneurysms before aortic correction in stable patients.</p>
        <p>Patel and Richardson reported 7 of 58 aneurysm patients with aortic coarctation. In one&#x000a0;series, 2 children presented with aortic stenosis and symptomatic cerebral aneurysms. Preadolescent patients with coarctation should undergo magnetic resonance angiography (MRA) or spinal CT angiography with 3-dimensional reconstructions. While less sensitive than standard angiography, MRA and CT provide safe, noninvasive, and effective screening. Normal results may warrant repeat surveillance at the clinician&#x02019;s discretion. Patients with clinical evidence of aneurysmal rupture or questionable MRA findings should undergo 4-vessel cerebral angiography. Specialized techniques, eg, brachial catheterization, may be required in cases of severe stenoses, underscoring the importance of an experienced neuroradiologist given the increased complexity in younger patients and contrast load considerations.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Polycystic Kidney Disease</bold>
</p>
        <p>Polycystic kidney disease presents in 2 distinct forms. Autosomal-dominant polycystic kidney disease (ADPKD) affects multiple organs, including the heart, gastrointestinal tract, and kidneys, and carries an increased risk of intracranial aneurysms, although reported prevalence ranges from 0% to 41%. Delayed diagnosis until adulthood contributes to this variability, with most aneurysm ruptures occurring after the second decade. Inconsistent use of angiography, fine-cut CT, and MRI/MRA further complicates incidence estimates, although isolated cases confirm aneurysms in infants.</p>
        <p>Adult ADPKD has been linked to a 100kb locus on chromosome 16, PKD1. Studies demonstrate up to 18% aneurysm incidence in PKD1 families with at least one affected member. PKD1 is also implicated in conditions associated with renal cysts, eg, tuberous sclerosis, and the arteriopathic similarities among ADPKD, fibromuscular dysplasia, and tuberous sclerosis suggest a potential common role in vessel wall development. Infantile polycystic kidney disease, inherited in an autosomal recessive manner, presents early as a flank mass or abdominal enlargement, with short life expectancy and extremely rare aneurysms. In one series of 14 patients, 12 died before adolescence without aneurysms, and only one survivor developed a basilar aneurysm at age 18.</p>
        <p>Chapman reported a 4% frequency of unruptured aneurysms in 92 ADPKD patients, compared with 1% in the general population (P&#x0003c;0.04, 95% confidence interval), using fine-cut CT with multi-planar reconstruction for initial screening and angiography to confirm suspicious findings. Despite its sensitivity and specificity, routine cerebral angiography in ADPKD patients remains uncommon due to cost, risk, and outcome considerations. Reported complication rates support cautious use of angiography. Angiographic studies suggest a higher frequency of unruptured aneurysms than CT screening alone, with 41% of 17 asymptomatic patients and 37% of 19 symptomatic patients demonstrating aneurysms.</p>
        <p>Based on these observations, a definitive statement cannot be made as to the exact risk of ADPKD patients harboring an intracranial aneurysm. Cerebral angiography, on the other hand, does carry a clear amount of risk in ADPKD patients. Current recommendations advise fine-cut CT or MRI/MRA screening for all patients diagnosed with ADPKD. Patients with neurological symptoms or abnormal noninvasive imaging should undergo 4-vessel cerebral angiography. Routine screening in infantile polycystic kidney disease remains unnecessary due to low risk and short life expectancy.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Tuberous Sclerosis</bold>
</p>
        <p>Tuberous sclerosis (Bourneville disease) has been associated with multiple intracranial aneurysms and renal cysts. This disorder exhibits protean manifestations affecting multiple organ systems and is most consistently explained as a developmental tissue dysplasia involving both ectodermal and mesodermal elements. The classic clinical presentation includes a triad of intellectual disability, epilepsy, and adenoma sebaceum.&#x000a0;The gene implicated in tuberous sclerosis shares a chromosomal locus with the polycystic kidney disease gene<italic toggle="yes">, PKD1</italic>&#x000a0;(100kb), on chromosome 16.&#x000a0;Blumenkopf, in his review of the literature, identified only&#x000a0;5 cases of tuberous sclerosis with concurrent intracranial aneurysms. The underlying tissue dysplasia likely contributes to congenital defects in the arterial wall, accounting for the clinical observation of increased numbers of multiple and fusiform aneurysms in affected patients.</p>
        <p>Histological findings in the renal cysts of tuberous sclerosis mirror those observed in patients with autosomal-dominant polycystic kidney disease. Both disorders may coexist in the same patient and can be difficult to distinguish in cases with nonclassic phenotypes. A central role for the&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">PKD1</italic>&#x000a0;gene in these histological changes has been proposed but remains unproven. Other congenital syndromes associated with renal cysts, eg, Zellweger disease and Meckel-Gruber syndrome, do not demonstrate an elevated risk of&#x000a0;cerebral aneurysms. Screening angiography in children with tuberous sclerosis has demonstrated limited efficacy, as aneurysmal lesions frequently rupture despite their absence on initial imaging.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Fibromuscular Dysplasia</bold>
</p>
        <p>Described primarily as an angiopathy often associated with hypertension, fibromuscular dysplasia (FMD) is characterized by dysplastic vessel wall changes that spread multifocally within branches of the aorta. Found primarily in hypertensive middle-aged women, FMD has been associated with an increased number of intracranial aneurysms as well as with multiple other congenital anomalies. Although the establishment of this linkage between FMD and cerebral aneurysms is clear in adult patients, cases of SAH and aneurysms in children with FMD are exceedingly rare.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Ehlers-Danlos and Marfan Syndrome</bold>
</p>
        <p>Intracranial aneurysms are most frequently associated with patients having type IV Ehlers-Danlos syndrome. As an autosomal-dominant defect in collagen synthesis, Ehlers-Danlos syndrome is characterized by hyperplastic skin and hyperextensible joints. As a clinical entity, it further separates into several clinical types (I-IV) with differences in the specific collagen synthesis defect, clinical features, and inheritance pattern evident. The classic phenotypical features are not typically seen in patients with type IV disease and thus can cause delayed diagnosis until rupture of the cerebral aneurysm occurs, usually in adulthood. Obtaining an MRI/MRA or CT of a child with known type IV disease would not be unwarranted. Intracranial aneurysms have also been occasionally reported in patients with Ehlers-Danlos type I and type VI&#x000a0;as well.</p>
        <p>Whereas the existence of large dissecting aneurysms of the aorta is a well-recognized feature of Marfan syndrome, less clear is the association between Marfan syndrome and intracerebral aneurysms. A review of the literature reveals that no definitive conclusion has been reached regarding the relationship between berry aneurysms and Marfan syndrome.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Familial Aneurysm Occurrence</bold>
</p>
        <p>Given the overall rare incidence of intracranial aneurysms in children, familial cases occur even less frequently. Although most affected patients remain asymptomatic until adulthood, familial aneurysms tend to rupture at an earlier age than expected. These lesions frequently involve the peripheral vasculature, particularly within the middle cerebral artery territory. Reports indicate an increased incidence of multiple aneurysms in familial cases, especially when associated with conditions, eg, Marfan syndrome, Ehlers-Danlos syndrome, polycystic kidney disease, hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia, cavernous angiomas, Von-Hippel-Lindau disease, arteriovenous malformations, and Sturge-Weber syndrome.</p>
        <p>Developing a rational, safe, and cost-effective strategy for screening children with a familial history has proven challenging. Angiography carries measurable risk in the pediatric population. Advances in imaging now allow screening with MRI/MRA or CT, which offers a safer alternative. Asymptomatic children with a single affected parent generally do not require routine screening due to relatively low risk. Aggressive evaluation may be warranted when multiple younger family members are affected, and standard angiography should be performed in all cases with clinical or radiographic suspicion.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-17551.s6" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>Numerous clinical conditions associated with cerebral aneurysms have been discussed previously. When these conditions are encountered, clinicians should maintain a heightened index of suspicion and implement careful surveillance. Overall, the presentation of intracranial aneurysms in children closely resembles that in adults, although several clinical features remain unique to the pediatric population. SAH represents the most common initial manifestation of cerebral aneurysms across age groups.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r33">[33]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r34">[34]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r35">[35]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r36">[36]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Subarachnoid Hemorrhage</bold>
</p>
        <p>Rates of SAH in children range from 1.9% to 4.6%, and increased recognition likely reflects advances in diagnostic imaging and heightened clinical awareness. Amacher and Drake reported that 65% of their 52 childhood cases had ruptured at diagnosis. Our own experience demonstrates a 74% incidence of SAH at presentation. Compared with adults, a greater proportion of childhood aneurysms are symptomatic at diagnosis. A recent retrospective review of nearly 1500 MRAs identified 64 aneurysms in 49 patients, of which only 2 (4%) presented with acute SAH, while headache emerged as the most common presenting symptom.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r37">[37]</xref>&#x000a0; Risk factors for rupture include age younger than 5 years, lesions in the anterior cerebral artery, posterior cerebral artery, or distal arterial regions. Wide-neck morphology correlates with lower rupture risk.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r38">[38]</xref></p>
        <p>The onset of SAH typically occurs suddenly, with headache, vomiting, altered consciousness, seizures, coma, paresis, cranial nerve palsies, fever, and meningismus commonly reported. Sentinel bleeds may precede major hemorrhage, manifesting as headache in 10% to 15% of patients. Children may also present with focal neurological deficits from aneurysm expansion, symptoms of mass effect, or cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) obstruction in giant aneurysms. These warning signs are often transient, making early detection challenging. Aneurysms remain the leading cause of atraumatic SAH in patients&#x000a0;younger than 20.</p>
        <p>Among children with ruptured aneurysms, estimated mortality after the first bleed ranges from 11% to 20%, compared with 20% to 30% in adults. In our series, 7% of ruptured aneurysms rebled, resulting in 25% subsequent mortality. Proust reported a 52.4% incidence of rebleed in 22 pediatric aneurysm patients. Larger series indicate an overall mortality of 13% to 34% for children with ruptured aneurysms.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r34">[34]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r35">[35]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r39">[39]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Mass&#x000a0;Effect</bold>
</p>
        <p>A large proportion of children present with signs and symptoms of a mass lesion. In our experience of 52 children with aneurysms, one-third presented with signs of increased intracranial pressure. This finding shows a special emphasis in certain clinical subgroups, including children younger than 5 years (56%, mass effect) and giant aneurysms (90%). Concurrently, hydrocephalus was observed in 30% of all cases. Giant aneurysms (56%), posterior circulation lesions (60%), and early childhood cases (younger than&#x000a0;5 years; [36%]) were particularly&#x000a0;prone to the development of obstructed CSF flow.</p>
        <p>Findings, eg, lethargy, nausea, vomiting, a tense, anterior fontanelle, splayed sutures, papilledema, opisthotonic posture, and paresis or plegia, can be present in children with mass effect related to an aneurysm.&#x000a0;Mass effect symptoms often correlate with&#x000a0;ICH in children with ruptured aneurysms. Intraparenchymal and interventricular hemorrhage can often occur&#x000a0;in conjunction&#x000a0;with the initial SAH, with an incidence of 30% to 40%.</p>
        <p>The predilection for childhood aneurysms at the internal carotid artery bifurcations and distal middle cerebral arteries is thought to be partially responsible for ICH at presentation. The presence of ICH is associated with a higher risk of mortality and an overall poorer prognosis. In&#x000a0;a study of 43 children, the overall mortality of cases with an ICH (50%) was twice that of children without ICH (24%).</p>
        <p>Enlargement of an aneurysm, hematoma formation, or hydrocephalus can lead to focal neurological findings due to cranial nerve parenchymal compression, including sixth-nerve palsies, focal third-nerve palsies,&#x000a0;ophthalmoplegia, dysphasia, and other lower cranial palsies. Hemiparesis, hemiplegia, and quadriparesis secondary to pyramidal compression may also be present. Amacher and Drake reported a 34.4% incidence of neural compression in 32 children.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r31">[31]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r40">[40]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r41">[41]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Seizures</bold>
</p>
        <p>Although seizures and epilepsy are often common for children with AVMs, they are comparatively uncommon with an unruptured aneurysm.&#x000a0;Focal or generalized seizures can occur in 15% to 25% of children with SAH and may be more common in infants. A 7% seizure rate at the time of presentation has been reported. Seizures occurred most frequently in children with giant (25%) and posterior (27%) circulation aneurysms. Other series have reported an incidence as high as 25% of seizures arising from possible microemboli from the aneurysmal sac itself. The majority of seizures occur within 24 hours of the initial hemorrhage, but sometimes progress to epilepsy or status epilepticus. Elevations of arterial blood pressure and the concurrent risk of rebleed&#x000a0;should be avoided by effective management with anticonvulsant medications. However, long-term seizure prophylaxis has generally&#x000a0;not been necessary.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r42">[42]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r43">[43]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-17551.s7" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>For asymptomatic children who are at increased risk for an intracranial aneurysm, a CT-angiogram or an MR-angiogram is a useful and reasonable first screening tool. As discussed earlier, children with a strong family history of aneurysms, Ehlers-Danlos syndrome, FMD, polycystic kidney disease, and other high-risk conditions are good candidates for MRA. MRI and MRA can visualize larger lesions of the circle of Willis, but cerebral angiography remains the gold standard for the localization of cerebral aneurysms.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r44">[44]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r45">[45]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>For ruptured aneurysms, lumbar puncture and CT/MR imaging&#x000a0;remain the mainstay in the initial evaluation of SAH. Approximately 85% of children with subarachnoid blood can be accurately identified with a noncontrast CT scan when performed within the first 2 days after hemorrhage, but becoming less reliable after 5 days.&#x000a0;CT or MRA is extremely&#x000a0;useful in localizing larger cerebral aneurysms. For such cases, detection of the mass lesion may be quite&#x000a0;easy, even on noncontrast screening studies. Contrast studies can provide additional clues to the rupture site. In very&#x000a0;young infants, intracranial ultrasound has also been successfully used to diagnose large cerebral aneurysms. Ultrasonography can also show hydrocephalus, hematoma, shift, and cerebral edema.</p>
        <p>Given the high incidence of elevated intracranial pressure in these children, deferring lumbar puncture until the exclusion of a mass lesion (eg, an aneurysm), hematoma, cerebral edema, and hydrocephalus on imaging is recommended. An apparent SAH on a head CT scan obviates the need for lumbar puncture. If the imaging study results are negative for SAH and no radiographic or clinical evidence of increased intracranial pressure is needed, CSF can be safely obtained to confirm suspicious cases.</p>
        <p>Once the presence of&#x000a0;SAH has been established in a child, a standard 4-vessel cerebral angiogram should be obtained. For children younger than 6 years, general anesthesia is often required, with careful attention paid to fluid balance, contrast load, blood pressure control, and neural protection. Although controversy exists about the optimal timing of the angiogram, studies have demonstrated no increased morbidity with early studies (performed within a few hours after SAH) compared with delayed studies. Indeed, some large series have actually&#x000a0;shown lower morbidity with early studies.</p>
        <p>Overall, cerebral angiography can demonstrate a structural cause for SAH in 50% to 70% of cases. Of the negative studies, another 10% to 20% will subsequently be positive for an aneurysm on a repeat study at 2 weeks. Focal arterial spasm and thrombosis within the aneurysmal neck may prevent filling of the lesion during angiography, resulting in negative studies.&#x000a0;When no aneurysm can be identified in a confirmed case of a traumatic SAH,&#x000a0;other differential diagnoses should&#x000a0;be considered (see <bold>Image</bold>. Pseudoaneurysm). Systemic childhood causes of SAH include leukemia, idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura, hemorrhagic disease of the newborn, various&#x000a0;hemophilia disorders, and other hemorrhagic diatheses.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r46">[46]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r47">[47]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-17551.s8" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>
<bold>Medical Management</bold>
</p>
        <p>In patients with SAH and suspected aneurysm, appropriate medical management should be initiated immediately to stabilize the child and to prevent potential secondary complications of hemorrhage, as rebleeding represents the most serious complication within the first 48 hours after rupture.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r48">[48]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r49">[49]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r50">[50]</xref>&#x000a0;Definitive diversion of the CSF is often indicated if hydrocephalus is present. The decision to place a catheter before definitive management of the aneurysm commences requires caution; placement of a ventricular catheter is correlated with rupture, thought to be due to the associated change in transluminal pressure from altered cerebrospinal fluid CSF dynamics.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r51">[51]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r52">[52]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r22">[22]</xref></p>
        <p>Paying strict attention to the&#x000a0;patient&#x02019;s volume status usually requires placement of an indwelling urinary catheter and arterial line.&#x000a0;Adequate analgesia and sedation to treat the associated anxiety and headache of SAH must be weighed against the need for frequent evaluation of the neurological examination. Allowing the parents to remain with young patients often helps alleviate the child&#x02019;s fear. Antihypertensive medications, including calcium channel blockers, beta-blockers, and vasodilators, may be required to control blood pressure.</p>
        <p>Antiepileptic agents are initially administered to prevent post-SAH seizures and the undesirable effects of secondary hypertension and hypoxia. Stool softeners, breathing treatments, and antiemetic medication may also help reduce fluctuations in intracranial pressure caused by straining, coughing, and vomiting.</p>
        <p>Calcium channel blockers,&#x000a0;eg, nimodipine, may reduce the incidence of&#x000a0;secondary ischemia associated with vasospasm. Angiographic vasospasm is less common in children and may be asymptomatic, and experience with nimodipine in children with SAH is limited. Diagnostic techniques are similar to those used in adults, including computed tomography angiography, magnetic resonance angiography, digital subtraction angiography, and screening with transcranial Doppler ultrasonography, with perfusion studies when available. In cases of clinical vasospasm, volume expansion and permitted hypertension should be instituted, along with neurointerventional techniques (eg, intra-arterial vasodilators and balloon angioplasty). When promptly recognized, outcomes are more favorable than in adults.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r53">[53]</xref></p>
        <p>Prevention and recognition of delayed medical issues, eg, gastrointestinal hemorrhage (complicates approximately 4% of patients), pulmonary edema, and hyperglycemia, are important. Aggressive pulmonary toilet should be implemented, with appropriate antibiotic therapy, in cases of suspected infection. Unrecognized hyponatremia (cerebral salt wasting) may exacerbate alterations in the level of consciousness, increase seizure activity, and worsen cerebral edema. Decreased plasma sodium may be present in up to 35% of SAH patients, with a peak incidence between days 2 and 10 after rupture. Extracellular volume assessment in these patients typically shows a hypovolemic state. In most cases of hyponatremia, sodium replacement (oral sodium supplements and hypertonic intravenous saline), combined with volume expansion (normal saline, colloids, and red cells), is often adequate and appropriate&#x000a0;therapy.</p>
        <p>
<bold> Surgical Management </bold>
</p>
        <p>Surgical and endovascular intervention remains the definitive treatment of cerebral aneurysms in children. Several clinical series have reported poorer overall outcomes with nonsurgical management. This improved outcome from surgery must be weighed against the increased rate of spontaneous aneurysm thrombosis that experience shows may occur in childhood lesions. In cases where partial thrombosis of the lesion has already occurred, a conservative course of observation and supportive care may be necessary.</p>
        <p>In cases of mycotic aneurysms, conservative treatment with antibiotic agents is a reasonable, although controversial, alternative to direct surgical repair in some cases. For children with multiple aneurysms and those with preexisting cardiac disease and endocarditis, the risk of surgery may be unacceptably high, even when weighed against the ongoing risk of a potentially devastating hemorrhage. After institution of antimicrobial therapy, lesions should be followed closely with serial angiography every 2 to 3 weeks. Failure to involute or progressive enlargement of lesions despite antibiotics is a strong indication for surgical intervention. CT-angiogram and MRA can&#x000a0;be utilized&#x000a0;to&#x000a0;assess the response of mycotic aneurysms to treatment.</p>
        <p>Pediatric patients are more likely than adults to demonstrate fusiform, giant, or otherwise abnormally shaped aneurysms, requiring creative and high-skilled techniques for surgical treatment, and clip ligation remains the mainstay of treatment. Endovascular treatment in the pediatric population has generally lagged behind that in adults in popularity, due to early evidence of reduced durability and children's long life expectancy. Aneurysm recurrence rates were higher after endovascular treatment (12.7%) compared with surgical treatment (3.9%).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r54">[54]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>A recent retrospective series in Europe acknowledged the advances in endovascular management; however, approximately half of their patients were treated with open surgical intervention.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r55">[55]</xref>&#x000a0;Newer endovascular techniques, eg, flow-diverting stents, have been described in pediatric patients with good effect.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r56">[56]</xref>&#x000a0;Some have employed these at centers for complex fusiform aneurysms, which would otherwise have no surgical option. A systematic literature review identified 48 studies, including 1,482 aneurysms in total (611 treated endovascularly; 656 surgically; 215 conservatively).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r54">[54]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>From a technical perspective, surgical management of aneurysms in children is quite&#x000a0;similar to that of adults. Special attention must be paid to the smaller size of anatomic structures and the decreased tolerance for temperature changes, blood loss, and large fluid shifts due to smaller physiologic reserve in children.&#x000a0;Due to the often giant size and unusual locations of pediatric aneurysms, surgical obliteration is frequently not straightforward. A review of the literature shows that direct clip ligation of the aneurysmal neck is possible in only 29.5% of cases. A&#x000a0;high percentage of pediatric patients require specialized and often innovative procedures to obliterate their lesions.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r57">[57]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r58">[58]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r21">[21]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r36">[36]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r59">[59]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-17551.s9" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>Differential diagnoses that should also be considered when evaluating patients with suspected pediatric cerebral aneurysm include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndrome</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Migraine</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Acute management of stroke&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Anisocoria</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Anterior circulation stroke&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Aphasia</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Apraxia and related syndromes&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Basilar artery thrombosis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Cardioembolic stroke&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Carotid ultrasound&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Cavernous sinus syndrome&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Cerebellar hemorrhage&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-17551.s10" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>Outcomes are generally favorable in pediatric patients. In our series, aneurysm recurrence or rerupture occurred in&#x000a0;5 (15.2%) patients, and 5 patients (15.2%) died due to sequelae of their aneurysms. Twenty-one patients (63.6%) had a good outcome (modified Rankin Scale score 0-2)&#x000a0;at the last follow-up.&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-17551.s11" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Mean aneurysm recurrence rates in the literature were 12.7% and 3.9% for endovascular and surgical treatment, respectively.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r54">[54]</xref>&#x000a0;In the modern era, endovascular management of pediatric cerebral aneurysms represents a viable alternative to open surgical obliteration.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r44">[44]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r60">[60]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r61">[61]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r18">[18]</xref>&#x000a0;The minimally invasive nature of the procedure, smaller associated fluid shifts and blood losses, and decreased surgical manipulation of brain tissue are all attractive features of interventional therapy. However, like open surgery, endovascular management carries with it several serious risks and potential complications that must be recognized. Vessel perforation, stroke, hemorrhage, and incomplete obliteration may all result after an intervention.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r62">[62]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r45">[45]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r63">[63]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-17551.r64">[64]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-17551.s12" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Deterrence of pediatric cerebral aneurysm&#x02013;related morbidity focuses on early identification of at-risk populations and prevention of rupture. Although many cases are linked to congenital or structural vessel abnormalities, clinicians can reduce risk through targeted screening of children with conditions such as connective tissue disorders, polycystic kidney disease, or aortic coarctation, as well as those with relevant family histories. Prompt evaluation of warning symptoms, including sudden severe headache or neurologic deficits, is critical to prevent delayed diagnosis and catastrophic hemorrhage. Careful management of secondary contributors, eg, hypertension, infection, or trauma, along with appropriate surveillance imaging in high-risk patients, supports early detection and timely intervention.</p>
        <p>Patient and family education plays a central role in improving outcomes and reducing complications. Caregivers should be informed about early warning signs of subarachnoid hemorrhage, the importance of adherence to follow-up imaging, and the need for urgent medical evaluation of new neurologic symptoms. Education should also address medication adherence, the avoidance of activities that increase intracranial pressure when clinically indicated, and an understanding of treatment options, including surgical and endovascular approaches. Shared decision-making, clear communication, and reinforcement of follow-up plans help ensure engagement, reduce anxiety, and promote safer long-term management.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-17551.s13" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Pediatric&#x000a0;cerebral aneurysms are rare but clinically significant, accounting for a small proportion of all aneurysms yet representing a leading cause of atraumatic subarachnoid hemorrhage in children. Unlike adults, pediatric cases are more strongly associated with congenital vessel wall defects, connective tissue disorders, trauma, and infection rather than acquired vascular risk factors. Pathophysiology involves structural&#x000a0;weaknesses, such as fragmentation of the internal elastic lamina and collagen abnormalities, which predispose&#x000a0;to aneurysm formation, growth, and rupture. Children commonly present with sudden headache, vomiting, altered consciousness, seizures, or signs of mass effect, including hydrocephalus and focal neurologic deficits. Prompt evaluation with CT or MRI, followed by cerebral angiography, is essential for diagnosis. Management includes stabilization, prevention of rebleeding and secondary injury, and definitive surgical or endovascular intervention, with attention to complications such as vasospasm, hyponatremia, and increased intracranial pressure.</p>
        <p>Interprofessional collaboration is critical to improving patient-centered outcomes, safety, and quality of care. Physicians and neurospecialists guide diagnosis and intervention, while primary care clinicians and advanced practice providers facilitate early recognition, risk assessment, and timely referral of high-risk patients. Nurses provide continuous neurologic monitoring, manage intracranial pressure precautions, and support family education. Pharmacists optimize medication therapy, including antihypertensives, anticonvulsants, and agents to mitigate vasospasm. Radiologists ensure accurate and timely interpretation of imaging, and critical care teams coordinate hemodynamic and neurologic stabilization. Effective communication, shared decision-making with families, and coordinated follow-up and surveillance strategies are essential for reducing complications, improving adherence to care plans, and supporting long-term neurologic recovery.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-17551.s14">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
